cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "disclosed: grading US higher-education institutions on what they disclose"
message: >-
  If you use this dataset or its findings, please cite it. Please also carry the coverage
  caveat with the numbers: the committed capture is 600 institutions across 13 states, not a
  national sample, and shares computed from it are not national figures.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Kelly-Reif
    given-names: Chelsea
repository-code: "https://github.com/ChelseaKR/disclosed"
abstract: >-
  Grades US higher-education institutions on what they disclose rather than on how they
  perform. Many tools report a college's graduation rate; none report how many colleges never
  reported one, or which fields quietly stopped being published. A field an institution did not
  report, a field suppressed to protect a small cohort, and a field whose true value is zero are
  three different facts that all render as 0 if handled carelessly, so every value routes through
  a single classifier that keeps them distinct. Suppressed and not-applicable fields leave the
  denominator rather than scoring zero, and an institution with an empty denominator receives no
  grade at all rather than a zero. Findings carry a peer group so a graded institution argues
  with stated evidence rather than with a constant, and per-field disclosure counts are committed
  on every run so that a field which stops being published leaves a record in version control.
keywords:
  - open data
  - data quality
  - missing data
  - higher education
  - College Scorecard
  - IPEDS
  - transparency
license: Apache-2.0
